fs/hfsplus: mount a volume that has no backup volume header

The iOS rootfs on `userdata` (the H815 phone) is an HFS+ volume with the
case-sensitive signature "HX" and 4096 byte blocks. The primary volume
header is at offset 1024, as the spec says, and it is a good one: the
allocation file and the catalog file are described in it, and the driver
reads them from there.

The volume has NO backup volume header: the place where the copy belongs
(the second to last sector of the volume) is a hole of zeroes, and so is
the end of the partition. The driver reads that hole, compares its
signature with the primary one, finds them different and refuses the
mount:

    hfsplus: invalid secondary volume header

The backup is only a spare copy of the primary header. The driver uses the
primary one for everything it needs (the block size, the catalog file, the
allocation file), and the backup only in the HFSPLUS_IOC_BLESS ioctl and
in the write-back path. A missing backup is therefore no reason to refuse
the mount.

A backup that IS there but carries another signature is still an
inconsistency, and is still refused: only the "there is no backup at all"
case is accepted.

The volume is mounted read-only, so nothing is written to `userdata`.

--- a/fs/hfsplus/wrapper.c
+++ b/fs/hfsplus/wrapper.c
@@ -234,8 +234,34 @@

 	error = -EINVAL;
 	if (sbi->s_backup_vhdr->signature != sbi->s_vhdr->signature) {
-		pr_warn("invalid secondary volume header\n");
-		goto out_free_backup_vhdr;
+		u16 bsig = be16_to_cpu(sbi->s_backup_vhdr->signature);
+		u16 psig = be16_to_cpu(sbi->s_vhdr->signature);
+
+		/*
+		 * A volume can have NO backup volume header at all: the copy
+		 * that belongs at the end of the volume is then a hole (all
+		 * zeroes) and the driver used to refuse the mount because
+		 * of it ("invalid secondary volume header").
+		 *
+		 * The backup is only a spare copy of the primary header.
+		 * The driver reads the primary one and uses it for
+		 * everything (block size, catalog file, allocation file),
+		 * and the backup is used only by the HFSPLUS_IOC_BLESS ioctl
+		 * and by the write-back path. A missing backup is therefore
+		 * no reason to refuse the mount, and refusing it means that
+		 * a volume written by a tool that does not write the backup
+		 * cannot be read at all.
+		 *
+		 * A backup that IS there but carries another signature is
+		 * still an inconsistency, and is still refused.
+		 */
+		if (bsig != HFSPLUS_VOLHEAD_SIG && bsig != HFSPLUS_VOLHEAD_SIGX) {
+			pr_warn("hfsplus: no backup volume header (signature 0x%04x), the primary one (0x%04x) is used alone\n",
+				bsig, psig);
+		} else {
+			pr_warn("invalid secondary volume header\n");
+			goto out_free_backup_vhdr;
+		}
 	}

 	blocksize = be32_to_cpu(sbi->s_vhdr->blocksize);
